Exposure to second-hand smoke
Mental Health & Substance Use
Proximity to active smokers, Chronic cough/wheezing, Frequent respiratory infections

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-022
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of wheezing and severe nocturnal cough in a non-smoker following heavy environmental exposure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute escalation of shortness of breath, audible wheezing, and a persistent, exhausting cough over the last 6 hours, preventing sleep. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess respiratory distress, perform auscultation, and provide acute bronchodilator therapy to prevent progression to respiratory failure prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My roommate had a party inside all weekend, and now my chest is so tight I can't catch my breath or stop coughing."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began gradually two days ago but worsened acutely tonight. The patient reports a history of living in close proximity to active smokers. They note frequent exacerbations of reactive airway disease and a chronic cough with respiratory irritation. There is an increased susceptibility to upper respiratory tract infections, alongside nasal congestion and chronic rhinitis. Tonight, following intense environmental exposure over the weekend, the patient developed severe conjunctival irritation, watery eyes, and pronounced wheezing or shortness of breath despite being a non-smoker. The patient complains of reduced exercise tolerance and extreme fatigue from the nocturnal cough. A persistent odor of stale tobacco smoke is evident on their clothing.
Functional Status: Currently unable to sleep or lie flat due to respiratory distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living environment directly exacerbates physical illness; high risk of recurrent acute presentations.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Mildly tachypneic, using accessory muscles mildly, speaking in short sentences, audible wheeze.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia. Respiratory exam shows widespread, high-pitched expiratory polyphonic wheezes bilaterally, with a prolonged expiratory phase. No crackles or focal dullness. Oropharynx is mildly erythematous. Eyes show bilateral conjunctival injection. Findings strongly correlate with proximity to active smokers, chronic cough/wheezing, and frequent respiratory infections.
Environmental Observations: The residence heavily smells of stale cigarette smoke; multiple overflowing ashtrays visible in the common area.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular respiratory medications; no prior formal asthma diagnosis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Exposure to second-hand smoke - Status: Exacerbated (Triggering acute reactive airway bronchospasm)
Differentials:

Acute Viral Bronchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, the sudden onset directly following a specific environmental smoke exposure, and the presence of widespread polyphonic wheeze typical of bronchospasm.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the gradual onset linked to smoke exposure, bilateral wheezing rather than focal pain, and normal oxygenation improving rapidly with bronchodilators.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of peripheral edema, jugular venous distension, or bibasilar crackles.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in acute, moderate respiratory distress with prominent bronchospasm secondary to heavy exposure to environmental tobacco smoke. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the tachypnea, inability to lie flat, and audible wheeze. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was essential to perform a clinical respiratory exam, definitively rule out infectious pneumonia or critical hypoxia, and immediately administer inhaled bronchodilator therapy. Delaying intervention until regular clinic hours posed an unacceptable risk of the bronchospasm worsening, potentially leading to critical respiratory fatigue or hypoxic failure.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Salbutamol via spacer (4 puffs, repeated once after 20 minutes) resulting in significant clinical improvement (RR decreased to 18, SpO2 increased to 98%). Prescribed a short course of oral Prednisolone 25mg daily for 3 days to reduce acute airway inflammation. Expected investigation results: elevated serum or salivary cotinine levels in a non-smoker.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Environmental Boundaries." Instruct the patient to designate their personal bedroom as a strict zero-smoke zone, using a draft excluder and opening the window daily. Reward the establishment of this boundary with improved sleep quality and reduced coughing. This uses environmental control to modify the physiological response.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil.
DME/Safety (Optional): Spacer device provided.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for formal spirometry and asthma management plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient becomes unable to speak in full sentences, if lips or fingers turn blue, or if the inhaler fails to provide relief for more than two hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute bronchospasm treated; ongoing exposure will continually trigger symptoms regardless of medication.
Clinical Course: The acute inflammation will subside over 3-5 days with steroids, but the airway remains highly reactive to smoke.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you use the puffer 6 times in an hour and are still struggling for breath, or if you feel exhausted from breathing, call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The second-hand smoke has irritated the lining of your lungs, causing the tubes to squeeze shut. The puffer opens them immediately, and the steroid tablets will reduce the swelling over the next few days.
Actionable Steps: Take the steroid tablets in the morning with food. Use the puffer every 4 hours as needed. You must negotiate a smoke-free living arrangement.

